articles.content_en:
The following property shall not be recognized as debtor's property:
(1) Property of others possessed or used by the debtor under contracts such as warehousing, custody, contract work, consignment sales, borrowing, deposit, or lease, or under other legal relationships;
(2) Property in which the debtor has not yet acquired ownership in a sale with retention of title;
(3) Property the ownership of which exclusively belongs to the State and may not be transferred;
(4) Other property that does not belong to the debtor in accordance with laws and administrative regulations.

Where the debtor makes the following individual repayments to creditors, and the administrator requests revocation pursuant to Article 32 of the Enterprise Bankruptcy Law, the People's Court shall not support them:
(1) payments made by the debtor for water fees, electricity fees, and the like to maintain basic production needs;
(2) payments of labor remuneration and personal injury compensation by the debtor;
(3) other individual repayments that benefit the debtor's property.

Before the acceptance of a bankruptcy application, where a creditor initiates the following lawsuits concerning the debtor's property, and the cases have not yet been concluded at the time of acceptance of the bankruptcy application, the People's Court shall suspend the trial:
(1) claims that the secondary debtor directly repays the debt to the creditor in place of the debtor;
(2) claims that the debtor's capital contributors, promoters, and directors and senior officers who have the duty to supervise shareholders' performance of capital contribution obligations, or other shareholders, directors, senior officers, actual controllers, etc. who assisted in the withdrawal of capital contributions, directly bear liability to the creditor for inadequate capital contribution or withdrawal of capital contributions;
(3) claims that the debtor's shareholders directly repay the debts owed by the debtor to the creditor on the ground that the personalities of the debtor's shareholders and the debtor are seriously confused;
(4) other individual repayment lawsuits concerning the debtor's property.
After the debtor is declared bankrupt, the People's Court shall, in accordance with Article 44 of the Enterprise Bankruptcy Law, render a judgment rejecting the creditor's claims. However, this is excepted where the creditor changes its claims in the first instance to request that the recovered property be turned over to the debtor's property.
Before the debtor is declared bankrupt, if the People's Court rules to reject the bankruptcy application or terminate the bankruptcy proceedings pursuant to Article 12 or Article 108 of the Enterprise Bankruptcy Law, the above suspended cases shall be resumed according to law.

Where the debtor has the circumstances provided in Article 2, Paragraph 1 of the Enterprise Bankruptcy Law, the following income obtained by the debtor's directors, supervisors, and senior officers by taking advantage of their positions shall be recognized by the People's Court as abnormal income provided in Article 36 of the Enterprise Bankruptcy Law:
(1) performance bonuses;
(2) wage-based income obtained under circumstances where wages of employees are generally in arrears;
(3) other abnormal income.
Where the debtor's directors, supervisors, and senior officers refuse to return the above debtor's property to the administrator, and the administrator claims that the aforesaid persons shall return it, the People's Court shall support it.
Claims formed by the debtor's directors, supervisors, and senior officers due to returning the abnormal income under items (1) and (3) of Paragraph 1 may be discharged as ordinary bankruptcy claims. Claims formed due to returning the abnormal income under item (2) of Paragraph 1, pursuant to Article 113, Paragraph 3 of the Enterprise Bankruptcy Law, shall be discharged as arrears of employees' wages to the extent calculated according to the average wage of the enterprise's employees; the portion exceeding the amount calculated according to the average wage of the enterprise's employees may be discharged as ordinary bankruptcy claims.

Where property of others in the possession of the debtor is illegally transferred to a third party, and the third party has acquired ownership in good faith pursuant to Article 311 of the Civil Code, and the original right holder is unable to take back the property, the People's Court shall handle the matter in accordance with the following provisions:
(1) if the transfer occurred before the acceptance of the bankruptcy application, the claim formed by the original right holder due to property loss shall be discharged as an ordinary bankruptcy claim;
(2) if the transfer occurred after the acceptance of the bankruptcy application, the debt arising from damage to the original right holder due to the administrator's or relevant personnel's performance of duties shall be discharged as a common benefit debt.

Where property of others in the possession of the debtor is illegally transferred to a third party, and the third party has paid the transfer price to the debtor, but has not acquired ownership pursuant to Article 311 of the Civil Code, and the original right holder recovers the transferred property according to law, the People's Court shall handle the debt arising from the third party's payment of consideration in accordance with the following provisions:
(1) if the transfer occurred before the acceptance of the bankruptcy application, the debt shall be discharged as an ordinary bankruptcy claim;
(2) if the transfer occurred after the acceptance of the bankruptcy application, the debt shall be discharged as a common benefit debt.

Article 43 Where a creditor claims set-off, and the administrator raises objection on the following grounds, the People's Court shall not support it:
(1) At the time of acceptance of the bankruptcy application, the debt owed by the debtor to the creditor was not yet due;
(2) At the time of acceptance of the bankruptcy application, the debt owed by the creditor to the debtor was not yet due;
(3) The subject matter of the mutual debts differs in kind and quality.

Article 46 Where a shareholder of the debtor claims to set off the following debts against the debts owed by the debtor to it, and the debtor's administrator raises an objection, the People's Court shall support it:
(1) Debts owed to the debtor by the shareholder due to the shareholder's failure to pay capital contributions or the withdrawal of capital contributions;
(2) Debts owed to the debtor by the shareholder due to the shareholder's abuse of shareholder rights or related-party relationships to harm the company's interests.
